<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="uracil degradation I (reductive)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3982" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Pyrimidine nucleotides can be catabolized through three different pathways. The best characterized is the reductive pathway (this pathway and |FRAME: PWY-6430|) in which the pyrimidine nucleotides are reduced to |FRAME:Beta-amino-acids|, |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| and |FRAME: AMMONIA|. The pathway is found in mammals, plants, some fungi and microorganisms |CITS:[13428755][13416173][16655478][16656066][10656811]|. The oxidative pathway (see |FRAME: PWY-6426|) is only found in a few bacterial species and has not been characterized nearly as well. In it uracil is converted to |FRAME: UREA| and |FRAME: MALONATE| via |FRAME: BARBITURATE| |CITS: [12981104][14955523]|. A third pathway, known as the Rut pathway, was discovered much later in |FRAME: TAX-562| and other bacteria (see |FRAME: PWY0-1471|) |CITS: [16540542]|. That pathway starts by ring opening of the pyrimidine nucleotide, and forms |FRAME: 3-HYDROXY-PROPIONATE| from uracil, with concomitant production of |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| and |FRAME: AMMONIA|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The pathway is found in mammals, plants, some fungi and microorganisms. It has been observed in various microorganisms including |FRAME: ORG-6124| |CITS: [13416173][13416174]|, |FRAME: TAX-12917| |CITS: [4986615]|, |FRAME: TAX-37762| |CITS: [3553866]|, |FRAME: TAX-287| |CITS: [1903745]|, |FRAME: TAX-329| |CITS: [ 7710277]|, |FRAME: TAX-316| |CITS: [Xu92]|, |FRAME: TAX-303| |CITS: [11759049]|, |FRAME: TAX-292| |CITS: [West97]|, |FRAME: TAX-90371| |CITS: [3926948]| and |FRAME: TAX-51101| |CITS: [12670488]|, as well as the yeast |FRAME: TAX-4934| |CITS: [10656811]| and mammalian tissues |CITS: [12981074][13428755]|. The product of the pathway, |FRAME: B-ALANINE|, is a central component of |FRAME:PANTOTHENATE| and |FRAME:GAMMA-BUTYROBETAINE| biosynthesis, and in mammals also functions as a neurotransmitter |CITS: [6117607]| and as a precursor for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CARNOSINE| and |FRAME: CPD-401| |CITS: [3108250]|. While in bacteria |FRAME: B-ALANINE| biosynthesis usually occurs through the decarboxylation of |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE| (see |FRAME: PWY-5155|), it is generally thought that the pyrimidine degradation pathway is the main route for the synthesis of |FRAME: B-ALANINE| in mammals |CITS: [14705962]|. Radiotracer experiments in germinating grains of |FRAME: TAX-4565| |CITS: [mazus72]|, whole plants of |FRAME: TAX-227291| |CITS: [duhaze03]| and potato tubers |CITS: [12244448]| showed that uracil is efficiently converted to |FRAME: B-ALANINE|. Mutant analyses in |FRAME: ORG-5993| further support the existence of this pathway and indicate that it might be important for helping plants to reallocate nitrogen from pyrimidines into general nitrogen metabolism |CITS: [19413687]| The pathway proceeds in three sequential enzymatic steps. The first enzyme is |FRAME: MONOMER-15398| (EC 1.3.1.2), which catalyzes the reversible reduction of both |FRAME:URACIL| and |FRAME: THYMINE| to |FRAME: DI-H-URACIL| and |FRAME: DIHYDRO-THYMINE|, respectively (see also the |FRAME: PWY-6430| pathway). The second enzyme, |FRAME: MONOMER-15399| (EC 3.5.2.2) performs reversible hydrolytic ring-opening of |FRAME: DI-H-URACIL| and |FRAME: DIHYDRO-THYMINE| to |FRAME: 3-UREIDO-PROPIONATE| and |FRAME: 3-UREIDO-ISOBUTYRATE|, respectively. Finally, |FRAME: MONOMER-15400| (EC 3.5.1.6) catalyzes the irreversible hydrolysis of |FRAME: 3-UREIDO-PROPIONATE| and |FRAME: 3-UREIDO-ISOBUTYRATE| to |FRAME: B-ALANINE| and |FRAME: 3-AMINO-ISOBUTYRATE|, respectively, in a reaction that produces ammonium and carbon dioxide. This pathway has special importance in humans, since it is responsible for the degradation of |FRAME: CPD0-1327|, an important anticancer drug. Patients with defects in the enzymes of this pathway show adverse response to treatment with this drug, including death |CITS: [993199]|.</Description>
