    <DataNode elementId="AT3G17810-MONOMER" textLabel="dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LVI9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Although the activity of this enzyme has not been characterized &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, mutant analyses indicate the importance of this enzyme for uracil catabolism in |FRAME: ORG-5993| |CITS: [19413687]|. Similar enzymes from animals and humans that have an additional N-terminal domain work exclusively with NADPH. The true co-substrate(s) of PYD1 have not yet been identified and it could potentially use NADH as a reducing agent as well |CITS: [19413687]|. Moreover, the lack of DHPDH activity &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; for PYD1 suggests that it may have a compulsory protein interaction partner that has not yet been discovered |CITS: [19413687]|. Mutants that lack a full-length functional copy of this gene have higher levels of uracil and decreased levels of CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; production from C14-labeled uracil. However, &lt;i&gt;pyd1&lt;/i&gt; mutant plants can survive on uracil as their sole nitrogen source |CITS: [19413687]|. Interestingly, many transcripts related to &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; pyrimidine biosynthesis are up-regulated in &lt;i&gt;pyd1&lt;/i&gt; mutants, while several other transcripts related to pyrimidine metabolism are also altered, suggesting potentially complex modes of transcriptional control of these biochemical pathways |CITS: [19413687]|. PYD1 transcript levels rise in response to reduced nitrogen levels. This finding, as well as mutant growth responses indicate that the uracil degradation pathway may be important for releasing nitrogen stored in pyrimidines for general nitrogen metabolism under conditions of low nitrogren |CITS: [19413687]|. It is possible that PYD1 may also help to catalyze the reduction of thymine to dihydrothymine but this has not been backed up with experimental support to date |CITS: [19413687]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G17810-MONOMER" />
